{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966787,"nonce":"8v3rE0qvilh9zmRNaELSA3qZs38=","title":"current_call_id() is ZERO for a DSL widget with children — the pass that brackets a lowered","message":"`current_call_id()` only reads correctly inside a\n`__push_call_id__` / `__pop_call_id__` bracket. `lower_component_calls`\nsays outright that the bracket injection is **deferred**: the ABI fns and\nthe `call_site_instance_id` helper exist, but nothing emits the wrap. So\nthe id reads **zero** for these widgets, and every instance in a program\ncollides on one key.\n\nThis is the same fact behind [[project-dsl-signal-driven-overlays]]. It\nbit again on `cn.RadioGroup`: keying per-option state on the call id put\nboth groups on `cn-radio-group-0`, so hovering an option in one drove a\nrebuild the other answered too — the dial took the wrong group's\ngeometry, its row changed height, and the two groups disagreed about\nwhat was selected while binding one signal.\n\n**A cn widget's own `InstanceKey` does not save you.** It is\n`#[track_caller]`, and every instance of a DSL widget is built from the\nONE `blinc_cn::foo(...)` line inside its wrapper, so they all share a\ncall site. `cn.Tabs` had the same latent collision for the same reason.\n\nKey on **what the author wrote**: the bound signal's id, plus the props\nthat distinguish instances (label, size, layout, and the child values).\nStable across rebuilds, different wherever two instances differ. Two\ninstances alike in every one of those respects still share state, so\ngive the widget an optional `key` prop as the way out.\n\nVerify before assuming a call id works — a two-line `eprintln` in the\nwidget's build, driven from a DSL test with two instances, settles it.","files":null}]}